[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Leaks & LeakCanary Diagnostics in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memory leaks in Android occur when an object that is no longer needed cannot be garbage collected because active references keep it alive in memory. Over time, these leaks accumulate, leading to increased Garbage Collection (GC) frequency, app sluggishness, and eventually the dreaded OutOfMemoryError (OOM)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Causes of Memory Leaks in Java"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static Context References: Storing an Activity or Context reference inside a static variable."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inner Classes and Handlers: Non-static inner classes (like Threads, Handlers, or AsyncTask) hold an implicit reference to their outer enclosing Activity."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unregistered Listeners and Callbacks: Registering a component to an event bus or system service without unregistering it in onDestroy()."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating LeakCanary"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"LeakCanary is a powerful memory leak detection library for Android developed by Square. To add it to your project, include the debug implementation dependency in your app-level build.gradle file."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"dependencies {
    debugImplementation 'com.squareup.leakcanary:leakcanary-android:2.12'
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Analyzing Leak Traces"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once integrated, LeakCanary runs automatically in debug builds. When an Activity or Fragment is destroyed but not garbage collected, LeakCanary dumps the heap, analyzes the retention path, and displays a notification with a detailed leak trace."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Fixing a Leak: Static Context Example"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider the following leak-prone Java singleton class holding a direct reference to an Activity Context."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public class LeakyManager {
    private static LeakyManager instance;
    private Context context;

    private LeakyManager(Context context) {
        this.context = context; // Leaks Activity Context!
    }

    public static synchronized LeakyManager getInstance(Context context) {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new LeakyManager(context);
        }
        return instance;
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To resolve this issue and satisfy LeakCanary, always use the Application Context for singletons or long-lived background objects."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class SafeManager {
    private static SafeManager instance;
    private Context context;

    private SafeManager(Context context) {
        this.context = context.getApplicationContext(); // Safe application context
    }

    public static synchronized SafeManager getInstance(Context context) {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new SafeManager(context);
        }
        return instance;
    }
}"}]
